<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saffron Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2007">2007</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saffron Revolution was a wave of protests in Myanmar in August–October 2007, named after the robes of the Buddhist monks who led them. Sparked by a sharp rise in fuel prices, the marches grew to tens of thousands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and other cities before the military government crushed them in late September, raiding monasteries and killing dozens of people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saffron-revolution.jpg" alt="Saffron Revolution" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: racoles · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2007_Myanmar_protests_7.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Before the protests, unease about the economy had been growing. Late in 2006 the price of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, eggs and cooking oil rose by 30 to 40 per cent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> counted Myanmar among the 20 poorest countries. Many, the United Nations among them, blamed the military government for the situation, pointing to the share of national income spent on the armed forces. Small demonstrations over prices, health and education took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> in February and April 2007, and some demonstrators were detained.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 15 August 2007 the government, the only seller of fuel, removed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> without warning. Diesel and petrol prices rose by 66 to 100 per cent, and the price of compressed natural gas for buses by 500 per cent within a week. Protests began on 19 August, and the authorities arrested 13 prominent dissidents, among them Min Ko Naing and Ko Ko Gyi. On 5 September troops broke up a demonstration at Pakokku and injured three monks. Younger monks held officials briefly hostage, demanded an apology and, when the military refused, withdrew religious services from the military and began marching.</p><p>On 22 September about two thousand monks marched through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Yangon</a> and ten thousand through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandalay">Mandalay</a>. In Yangon they chanted the Metta Sutta past the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a>, who was under house arrest and appeared at her gate to accept their blessings. On 26 September troops barricaded the Shwedagon Pagoda and attacked some 700 protesters with batons and tear gas, yet monks went on marching in the following days. The government tried to block websites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/email">email</a>, but bloggers in Yangon still got photographs and video out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The number of dead is not known. The official toll was 13, among them the Japanese photojournalist Kenji Nagai, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> envoy Paulo Sérgio Pinheiro confirmed 31. Pinheiro also reported independent sources who counted 30 to 40 monks and 50 to 70 civilians killed, while the Democratic Voice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> gave 138. State media said 2,100 people had been arrested by 11 October, but foreign sources put the number held above 6,000. In November 2008 a court sentenced 14 members of the 88 Generation Students Group to 65 years in prison. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> held to non-interference.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saffron Revolution” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Saffron Revolution</b> led by Buddhist monks in 2007 was suppressed, and in May 2008 Cyclone Nargis killed about 138,000 people in the Irrawaddy delta while the regime initially blocked foreign aid.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
